WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.557 --> 00:00:02.925
[Helicopter Whirring]

2
00:00:02.995 --> 00:00:04.931
[Phone Ringing]

3
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.134
Where are those projections?
Three, six and nine
are in their folders.

4
00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.678
Can I have some more-
Excellent.
Tea's at your seat. Hello.

5
00:00:11.747 --> 00:00:14.651
Oh, hold on.

6
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.521
Ben Federman again,
another pre-call.

7
00:00:17.592 --> 00:00:22.193
[Phone Beeps]
We did the pre-call.
Is this the post pre-call call?

8
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.797
[Federman]
The board's gonna want specifics
with the push into the Asian markets.

9
00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:26.811
Ben, there are no specifics
about Asia.

10
00:00:26.879 --> 00:00:29.406
We came up with that
as a Hail Mary at 3:00 in the morning...

11
00:00:29.485 --> 00:00:31.478
to placate the board
about international.

12
00:00:31.556 --> 00:00:33.617
[Federman]
Carly, I'm just giving you
a heads-up about Asia.

13
00:00:34.963 --> 00:00:36.899
There is no Asia.

14
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.306
Let's talk about Asia.

15
00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:44.576
We're in the preliminary stages
of forming a strategic alliance
with Credit Lyonnais.

16
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:48.920
Within three months,
you won't be able to walk four feet
in Kangham, South Korea...

17
00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:50.928
without seeing
one of our beautiful models...

18
00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:55.233
smiling at you from billboards
and drugstore windows,
inviting you in.

19
00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:58.938
That's incredible news, Carly.
We'd hoped to hear
something about Asia.

20
00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:01.609
Had no idea
plans were so far along.

21
00:01:01.685 --> 00:01:04.452
Well, you know Asia.
Nothing's done until it's done.

22
00:01:04.526 --> 00:01:08.056
- [All Laughing]
- [Man]
Carly, I was wondering,

23
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.626
could you walk us through
what you were thinking
for other Asian territories?

24
00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:14.108
Absolutely. India has over
half a billion women.

25
00:01:14.178 --> 00:01:18.084
In terms of spending power,
it's the single largest potential market
in the world.

26
00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:31.777
Um, we have retained a local firm
to ensure that cultural differences
are respected.

27
00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:35.846
Um, however, I can't
go into the specifics of my plan
with you until tomorrow.

28
00:01:35.924 --> 00:01:37.860
Right now I have a meeting
and I need the room.

29
00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:40.263
We were just getting started.

30
00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:43.534
I know and I do apologize.
It's last minute,

31
00:01:43.608 --> 00:01:47.103
but the meeting is with
three state department officials
to smooth the way for China.

32
00:01:47.182 --> 00:01:50.552
China?
This is just incredible.

33
00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:53.059
- We'll be in touch.
- Thank you.

34
00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:56.664
What is it?
I need a doctor.

35
00:01:56.736 --> 00:01:59.332
I can't move my leg.

36
00:02:36.251 --> 00:02:41.023
32-year-old female, paralysis
and severe pain in her right quad. Go.

37
00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:45.469
- How'd she get to you?
- She's the C.E.O. Of Sonyo Cosmetics.

38
00:02:45.538 --> 00:02:48.908
Three assistants and 15 V.P.'s
checked out who should be treating her.

39
00:02:48.979 --> 00:02:52.008
Who da man? I da man.
I always suspected.

40
00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:55.580
Dr. House, I know
the chances are very slim,
but I'm sure you recognize...

41
00:02:55.659 --> 00:02:58.790
that she may have what you had,
a clot in her thigh.

42
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:00.928
A bit of a long shot.

43
00:03:01.004 --> 00:03:04.033
- What about a disk herniation?
- I don't know, Eric.

44
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.378
If her disk were herniated,
she'd present with pain elsewhere,
wouldn't she?

45
00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.613
- Yeah, I suppose.
- You're right.

46
00:03:10.691 --> 00:03:13.561
A clot's also the most deadly,
right, Robert?

47
00:03:15.134 --> 00:03:18.436
True. The clot breaks off,
she could stroke and die.

48
00:03:18.507 --> 00:03:20.409
Dr. House,
I believe that they're right-

49
00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:23.645
- Stop talking.
- What?

50
00:03:23.718 --> 00:03:27.122
You read one of those
negotiating books, didn't you?

51
00:03:27.193 --> 00:03:32.796
Getting To Yes.
Fifty Ways to Win an Argument.
Hitchhiker's Guide to Being a Pal.

52
00:03:32.871 --> 00:03:37.369
In five seconds,
you just manipulated these two
into agreeing with your point of view.

53
00:03:37.447 --> 00:03:39.781
Fellas, this is known
as soft positional bargaining.

54
00:03:41.356 --> 00:03:43.326
It's not gonna work.

55
00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:45.863
Dr. House, are you saying
that she doesn't have a clot,

56
00:03:45.932 --> 00:03:49.063
or are you saying
that if she does have a clot,

57
00:03:49.138 --> 00:03:51.370
she doesn't need blood thinners
and an angiogram?

58
00:03:53.414 --> 00:03:57.320
Chase, put her on blood thinners.
Do an angiogram.

59
00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.952
When that comes back negative,
M.R.I. The spine.

60
00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:02.693
If that's clean,
cut her open and biopsy the leg.

61
00:04:02.768 --> 00:04:07.904
Excellent suggestion.
Read less, more TV.

62
00:04:07.978 --> 00:04:10.950
[Cuddy]
It's rare for an individual
to make a donation...

63
00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:16.360
significant enough
to impact an organization
as large and unwieldy as a hospital.

64
00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.960
This donation
does come with one string,

65
00:04:20.038 --> 00:04:22.201
that he be made
chairman of the board.

66
00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:26.010
I think that's a reasonable request.
I think he should have the right to know...

67
00:04:26.083 --> 00:04:30.957
what it is we do
with his $ 100 million.

68
00:04:31.027 --> 00:04:33.623
Please welcome
our new chairman of the board,
Edward Vogler.

69
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:37.902
- [Applause]
- Thank you.

70
00:04:37.976 --> 00:04:40.640
Thank you.

71
00:04:40.715 --> 00:04:44.085
[Sighs] When I was 18,

72
00:04:44.155 --> 00:04:46.250
my dad loaned me $20,000
for a college tuition,

73
00:04:46.326 --> 00:04:51.736
which he would have known was a mistake
had he known I wasn't actually in college.

74
00:04:51.804 --> 00:04:57.442
[Sighs] I took his money and invested
in a friend who had a little business.

75
00:04:57.516 --> 00:05:00.454
And when my dad found out
what I had done with his money,

76
00:05:00.523 --> 00:05:03.984
he and I didn't talk much
after that.

77
00:05:04.064 --> 00:05:06.000
But my friend's business took off,

78
00:05:06.068 --> 00:05:10.669
and I used the profits from that
to buy another company and another.

79
00:05:10.744 --> 00:05:13.340
And I must have been pretty good at it,
had a good eye,

80
00:05:13.417 --> 00:05:16.753
because before I knew it,
people were making offers for my company.

81
00:05:16.824 --> 00:05:19.454
And about a year ago,
I went public,

82
00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.764
and overnight
I was worth a billion dollars.

83
00:05:22.836 --> 00:05:25.272
So, I went to see my dad.

84
00:05:25.341 --> 00:05:29.748
I'll admit. I wanted a little payback.
You know, kind of shove
the win in his face.

85
00:05:29.818 --> 00:05:33.154
So, I drove upstate,
and sat in the kitchen I grew up in,

86
00:05:33.225 --> 00:05:36.664
and, uh, he had no reaction.

87
00:05:38.603 --> 00:05:42.668
It wasn't his fault.
He didn't even know who I was,

88
00:05:42.745 --> 00:05:46.115
because his Alzheimer's
had taken a turn for the worse,

89
00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:48.986
despite the best drugs
and care out there.

90
00:05:49.059 --> 00:05:51.621
And that is why I'm here.

91
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.968
What if my contribution to this hospital
is the difference between...

92
00:05:56.039 --> 00:05:58.909
no cure and a cure for cancer?

93
00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.182
The difference between
a man not recognizing
his wife of 35 years...

94
00:06:03.255 --> 00:06:08.527
and being able to look at her and say,
"Good morning, honey. I love you."

95
00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.974
If there's a disease out there
killing people,

96
00:06:13.042 --> 00:06:16.948
I am writing you a blank check
to fight back.

97
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:22.992
So, things are going to change a lot.

98
00:06:29.945 --> 00:06:31.709
[Mouthing Words]

99
00:06:33.319 --> 00:06:35.653
We're gonna have to ask you
for the cell phone.

100
00:06:35.723 --> 00:06:38.490
Do what you need to do.
I'm okay.

101
00:06:38.563 --> 00:06:41.694
I'm pretty sure my X-ray machine
can take your phone in a fight.

102
00:06:42.771 --> 00:06:45.766
It'll fry it.

103
00:06:45.845 --> 00:06:48.281
Fine.

104
00:06:50.822 --> 00:06:53.088
- How old is she?
- Thirty-two.

105
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.291
Wow. And she's already
the C.E.O. Of a public company.

106
00:06:56.367 --> 00:07:00.068
She's a workaholic.
Okay, Carly, hold still.

107
00:07:00.142 --> 00:07:02.374
The X-ray machine
is gonna pass over your leg.

108
00:07:02.446 --> 00:07:05.110
[Carly] Okay.
What'd you do with your time off?

109
00:07:05.185 --> 00:07:07.246
Snowboarding in Gstaad.

110
00:07:07.324 --> 00:07:09.260
Switzerland.

111
00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:11.161
Do you ski or board?
You can come with if you like.

112
00:07:11.232 --> 00:07:14.899
Maybe we should start with a drink
before we go around the world.

113
00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:18.139
Oh, you wanna have
a drink with me?

114
00:07:18.213 --> 00:07:21.185
Oh, very aggressive.
I like that.

115
00:07:26.597 --> 00:07:28.726
I wanna run this place
like a business.

116
00:07:28.802 --> 00:07:30.704
What, you want to put
more vending machines
in the hallway?

117
00:07:30.773 --> 00:07:33.643
Maybe a roulette wheel?
Nice one.
But I'm serious.

118
00:07:33.713 --> 00:07:37.311
The product that
you're selling is good health.
It shouldn't be a tough sell.

119
00:07:37.387 --> 00:07:40.826
If you don't wanna sell,
it means you don't care
if people get your product.

120
00:07:40.894 --> 00:07:44.458
You care if people are healthy,
or are you too proud for that?

121
00:07:47.308 --> 00:07:49.369
Who's that?

122
00:07:53.621 --> 00:07:56.855
Oh, that's just
one of our doctors.

123
00:07:56.928 --> 00:08:00.698
Aren't, uh, doctors
supposed to wear lab coats?

124
00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.104
- He's different.
- Everyone's buddy.

125
00:08:03.175 --> 00:08:07.275
- No, not exactly.
- Then why does he get away with it?

126
00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:12.190
It's just a coat.
He's very good.

127
00:08:13.263 --> 00:08:15.233
Hmm.

128
00:08:17.839 --> 00:08:20.275
- Say '"Ah."
- Ah.

129
00:08:20.344 --> 00:08:23.748
Now really belt it out,
like you're gonna throw up.
Ahh!

130
00:08:23.818 --> 00:08:27.656
[Coughs]
Perfect.

131
00:08:27.726 --> 00:08:29.992
Okay, that's it.

132
00:08:30.064 --> 00:08:33.559
We should know in a couple of days
what's growing in your son's throat.

133
00:08:36.344 --> 00:08:38.473
Hello?

134
00:08:38.549 --> 00:08:41.988
- He can't talk.
- Excuse me?

135
00:08:42.057 --> 00:08:45.188
- He had knee surgery.
- Right?

136
00:08:45.263 --> 00:08:47.961
About a year ago,
and then he couldn't talk.

137
00:08:48.035 --> 00:08:51.166
Right.
Yeah, well, that happens.

138
00:08:51.243 --> 00:08:55.776
You know, it's very dangerous
operating so close to the vocal cords.

139
00:08:55.852 --> 00:09:00.817
Okay. Well, we'll send
your kid's culture to the lab
and somebody will call you.

140
00:09:00.897 --> 00:09:03.163
- Boo!
- [Boy Yells]

141
00:09:06.608 --> 00:09:11.710
Just wanted to see if your dad,
you know- Bizarre.

142
00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.557
I need you to wear
your lab coat.

143
00:09:17.631 --> 00:09:22.539
I need two days of outrageous sex
with someone obscenely younger
than you, like half your age.

144
00:09:22.609 --> 00:09:24.738
Wear the coat.

145
00:09:24.813 --> 00:09:27.751
Man, oh, man,
someone got spanked
real good this morning.

146
00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.553
A guy gives $100 million
to cure cancer.

147
00:09:30.626 --> 00:09:33.028
A pretty small concession
to wear a lab coat.

148
00:09:33.097 --> 00:09:35.226
Cure cancer?

149
00:09:35.302 --> 00:09:38.297
Is the hospital getting
out of the dull business
of treating patients?

150
00:09:38.375 --> 00:09:40.504
You know that's not
what he's doing.
I know exactly what he's doing.

151
00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.709
He's using us to run
clinical trials.

152
00:09:42.784 --> 00:09:46.223
Oh, shame on him,
saving lives like that.

153
00:09:46.292 --> 00:09:49.162
It's unethical.

154
00:09:49.231 --> 00:09:52.362
Oh, are you coming in too?
I thought I had you convinced.

155
00:09:52.438 --> 00:09:54.931
Clinical trials
save thousands of lives.

156
00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:57.004
He's using patients as guinea pigs.

157
00:09:57.081 --> 00:09:59.745
Pharmaceutical companies
do that every day.

158
00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.020
Are we a pharmaceutical company?
We're gonna wind up pressuring
desperate patients...

159
00:10:03.094 --> 00:10:05.291
into choices that are
bad for them, good for us.

160
00:10:05.365 --> 00:10:08.826
We're gonna compromise
patient care.
Who the hell am I talking to?

161
00:10:08.906 --> 00:10:11.274
Suddenly ethical lapses
are a major concern for you?

162
00:10:12.347 --> 00:10:14.840
What's interesting is
it suddenly doesn't bother you.

163
00:10:16.288 --> 00:10:20.126
So, if you ignore ethics
to save one person it's admirable,

164
00:10:20.197 --> 00:10:22.360
but if you do it to save
a thousand, you're a bastard.

165
00:10:22.435 --> 00:10:25.703
All he's done is taken your game
and gone pro.

166
00:10:27.746 --> 00:10:31.686
He's not gonna kill a few patients.
He's gonna kill this hospital.

167
00:10:31.755 --> 00:10:34.955
It took him three seconds
to size you up.

168
00:10:35.028 --> 00:10:37.829
And surprise,
he doesn't like you.

169
00:10:38.902 --> 00:10:41.031
Wear the damn coat.

170
00:10:47.354 --> 00:10:51.522
Hello. I'm, uh, Dr. Foreman.
I work with Dr. House.

171
00:10:51.597 --> 00:10:53.533
Our initial tests say you're fine.

172
00:10:53.601 --> 00:10:56.698
We think you had a clot,
but it resolved on its own.

173
00:10:56.774 --> 00:11:00.304
So, we're gonna keep you
overnight to be safe,
and you can go back home tomorrow...

174
00:11:00.381 --> 00:11:02.908
or back to work.

175
00:11:03.989 --> 00:11:06.619
Hey, are you okay?
[Sobbing]

176
00:11:06.694 --> 00:11:09.928
[Screams]

177
00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:13.965
Get in here!
I need a line in her.
I.V. Morphine, stat!

178
00:11:15.046 --> 00:11:20.079
[Screams, Sobbing]

179
00:11:25.601 --> 00:11:27.503
You get any read
on the new chairman of the board?

180
00:11:27.573 --> 00:11:30.841
- Yeah, he took your parking space.
- It's not necessarily bad news.

181
00:11:30.912 --> 00:11:33.109
Do you ever watch
Gilligan's Island reruns...

182
00:11:33.184 --> 00:11:36.088
and really, really think
they're gonna get off
the island this time?

183
00:11:38.294 --> 00:11:40.264
We should introduce ourselves.
It couldn't hurt.

184
00:11:40.333 --> 00:11:45.640
Make him a Bundt cake.
A patient hit a 10 on the pain scale.
What would explain that?

185
00:11:45.711 --> 00:11:47.806
There was no clot in her leg.
The angio was totally clean.

186
00:11:47.882 --> 00:11:49.578
What about the muscle biopsy?

187
00:11:49.652 --> 00:11:51.884
No neurogenic
or myopathic abnormalities.

188
00:11:51.957 --> 00:11:56.797
She was also negative for trichinosis.
No toxoplasmosis
or polyarteritis nodosa.

189
00:11:56.867 --> 00:11:59.269
Robert, what was
her sedimentation rate?

190
00:12:00.408 --> 00:12:02.344
Normal, Allison.

191
00:12:02.412 --> 00:12:05.248
Therefore, no inflammation,
no immunologic response.

192
00:12:05.318 --> 00:12:07.914
Do you mind sharing
that number with me?

193
00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.983
Fifteen, Allison.

194
00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:13.330
- Are you mocking me?
- Duh, Allison.

195
00:12:13.402 --> 00:12:17.433
I'm just suggesting
we look outside the box.
What ifher sed rate is elevated?

196
00:12:17.511 --> 00:12:21.611
Well, let's go further outside the box.
Let's say the angio revealed a clot.

197
00:12:21.687 --> 00:12:23.987
And then let's say
we treated that clot,

198
00:12:24.058 --> 00:12:26.153
and now she's all better
and personally thanked me
by performing-

199
00:12:26.229 --> 00:12:28.131
My Aunt Elyssa
lives in Philadelphia.

200
00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:32.140
Oh, it's story time.
Let me get my "baba."

201
00:12:32.208 --> 00:12:36.308
Her normal temperature is 96.2,
not 98.6 like you and me.

202
00:12:36.384 --> 00:12:38.752
If her temperature were 98.6,
she'd have a fever.

203
00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:44.825
I'm just wondering if you think
we could apply the same logic
to Carly's sed rate.

204
00:12:46.605 --> 00:12:49.167
It's absurd.

205
00:12:49.244 --> 00:12:51.771
I love it.

206
00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:54.548
[Cameron]
If 15 is high for Carly,
then she has inflammation.

207
00:12:54.622 --> 00:12:57.924
Which could, in turn, mean cancer.
I'll talk to Wilson.

208
00:12:58.931 --> 00:13:02.097
Next time, skip Aunt Elyssa.

209
00:13:02.172 --> 00:13:04.642
You're probably talking about
a primary bone cancer.

210
00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.976
It could be tricky to detect.
We'll need a bone scan.

211
00:13:07.049 --> 00:13:09.212
That's why I'm talking
to an oncologist.

212
00:13:09.286 --> 00:13:14.285
Sure. I've nothing better to do
besides departmental meetings
and budget reports.

213
00:13:14.364 --> 00:13:17.962
New chairman of the board,
you know.
Oh, I hadn't heard.

214
00:13:18.038 --> 00:13:20.908
Right. Clinical trials.
Completely unethical.

215
00:13:20.977 --> 00:13:25.247
And a very bad omen for you.
There's not much money in curing
African sleeping sickness.

216
00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.417
No. I have seen
every scary movie ever made.

217
00:13:28.494 --> 00:13:30.555
Six-year-old twins
in front of an elevator of blood.

218
00:13:30.631 --> 00:13:32.863
Boys' choirs.
Those are bad omens.

219
00:13:32.937 --> 00:13:37.037
This is much more mundane.
A billionaire wants to get laid.

220
00:13:37.112 --> 00:13:39.344
Billionaires buy movie studios
to get laid.

221
00:13:39.417 --> 00:13:41.353
They buy hospitals
to get respect.

222
00:13:41.421 --> 00:13:44.552
And the reason you want respect?
To get laid.

223
00:13:44.628 --> 00:13:46.962
Okay then.
Just gotta think like a billionaire.

224
00:13:47.032 --> 00:13:49.696
Let's see.
There'll be big,
scary changes and then,

225
00:13:49.771 --> 00:13:54.873
"Oh, Dr. Cameron, we should have dinner
to discuss your future on my G5 private jet."

226
00:13:54.949 --> 00:13:57.249
Come on. You know
how good you have it here.

227
00:13:57.321 --> 00:14:00.623
Yes, I'm the big poobah,
the big cheese, the go-to guy.

228
00:14:00.695 --> 00:14:03.097
You do the cases you wanna do
when you wanna do them.

229
00:14:03.167 --> 00:14:05.694
You're not gonna have that
anywhere else.
Relax.

230
00:14:05.772 --> 00:14:08.368
I've been through
three regime changes in this hospital.
Every time same story.

231
00:14:08.444 --> 00:14:11.006
Just keep your head down.
That's all I'm saying.

232
00:14:11.083 --> 00:14:14.578
And put on your coat.
It itches.

233
00:14:14.658 --> 00:14:18.119
[Sighs]
So, you gonna do this bone scan
for me, or what?

234
00:14:18.199 --> 00:14:20.362
Yes.

235
00:14:30.959 --> 00:14:36.266
Dr. Simpson, did you hear?
New management.

236
00:14:36.337 --> 00:14:38.739
I'm thinking of switching
to orthopedics.

237
00:14:38.809 --> 00:14:42.806
How much do you guys
get for a massage now?
Without the happy ending.

238
00:14:42.883 --> 00:14:46.413
Dr. House, what do you want?
Do you remember a guy
named Van Der Meer?

239
00:14:46.491 --> 00:14:48.825
Not a big talker.
You fixed his A.C.L.

240
00:14:48.896 --> 00:14:50.866
No, not according
to my medical malpractice premiums.

241
00:14:52.437 --> 00:14:55.067
Didn't get hypotensive
during surgery?

242
00:14:55.143 --> 00:14:57.944
No strokes?
Maybe some connectivity loss?

243
00:14:58.016 --> 00:15:00.749
What, you're gonna
get involved now?
I'm not involved.

244
00:15:00.821 --> 00:15:04.055
A guy brought his son
into the clinic.
I didn't touch the son.

245
00:15:04.129 --> 00:15:06.793
I'm not taking
any responsibility there.
The son's fine. Can't shut him up.

246
00:15:06.868 --> 00:15:10.569
Dad show any symptoms
of cortical disease? Wernicke's?

247
00:15:12.112 --> 00:15:14.446
No. Nothing.

248
00:15:14.518 --> 00:15:17.456
That's why we settled
because we couldn't find anything.

249
00:15:17.524 --> 00:15:19.756
The guy got over a million dollars.
Don't tell me he's complaining.

250
00:15:19.828 --> 00:15:22.321
He's not saying boo.

251
00:15:26.509 --> 00:15:28.741
Your father wants to know
when you'll be back from your trip.

252
00:15:28.814 --> 00:15:32.184
E-mail back
it's taking longer than I thought.

253
00:15:32.254 --> 00:15:35.124
He doesn't need to
see me like this.
What about your brother?

254
00:15:35.195 --> 00:15:39.135
- No.
- Hello. I'm Dr. Wilson. I was-

255
00:15:39.203 --> 00:15:41.571
- Um, Robin, I'm gonna need a minute.
- Oh.

256
00:15:43.645 --> 00:15:46.309
Thank you.
[Clears Throat]

257
00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:54.498
There are two Dr. Wilsons
in this hospital,

258
00:15:54.568 --> 00:15:57.301
one in ophthalmology
and one in cancer.

259
00:15:57.374 --> 00:16:02.407
My eyes are fine,
so I'm guessing you're here
to tell me that I have cancer.

260
00:16:02.485 --> 00:16:05.149
There is no cancer in your bone.

261
00:16:05.224 --> 00:16:07.160
You're not smiling.

262
00:16:08.532 --> 00:16:12.165
There's something called referred pain.
You could have cancer
in one part of your body...

263
00:16:12.239 --> 00:16:14.505
that presents in another.

264
00:16:14.577 --> 00:16:17.549
Given your age
and your family history,
I'm thinking your colon.

265
00:16:17.617 --> 00:16:19.712
[Scoffs]
Great.

266
00:16:22.694 --> 00:16:26.327
I was at Columbia
when my mom died.
Now there's a blast.

267
00:16:26.402 --> 00:16:29.169
Cleaning up her vomit,
and then running to my econ final.

268
00:16:29.242 --> 00:16:31.371
Look.
[Chuckles]

269
00:16:31.446 --> 00:16:34.316
If I'm a short-timer, give me drugs.
I'll go back to work. I'll die there.

270
00:16:34.385 --> 00:16:38.485
Whoa. There's a very quick test
to see if you even have it-
a colonoscopy.

271
00:16:38.561 --> 00:16:41.658
[Chuckles]
I know how you do that test.

272
00:16:41.734 --> 00:16:45.401
If you have colon cancer,
we can treat it. It's early.

273
00:16:45.476 --> 00:16:49.006
That's what they told my mom.
She was dead six months later.

274
00:16:52.056 --> 00:16:55.689
You're a smart person
about to make a very bad decision.

275
00:16:55.764 --> 00:16:58.861
You know, cancer treatment's
come a long way in 12 years,
but if you don't do this now-

276
00:16:58.938 --> 00:17:01.374
I don't wanna be looked at.

277
00:17:10.028 --> 00:17:13.661
There is another way.

278
00:17:13.735 --> 00:17:16.171
We could do a virtual colonoscopy.

279
00:17:16.241 --> 00:17:20.774
Basically, we do a C. T. Scan
of your colon. It's noninvasive.

280
00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:23.981
But it's very expensive.
I'm assuming that's not a problem.

281
00:17:28.934 --> 00:17:30.870
Say yes.

282
00:17:34.512 --> 00:17:36.482
[Woman On P.A.]
Dr. Becker-

283
00:17:38.387 --> 00:17:40.254
Mr. Van Der Meer.

284
00:17:40.324 --> 00:17:42.521
[Typing]
What?

285
00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:49.903
Relax.
Ricky's gonna be just "finkf."
Strep throat.

286
00:17:49.978 --> 00:17:53.473
Here's a prescription
for an antibiotic. He should
be all better in a few days.

287
00:17:53.552 --> 00:17:57.515
Although,
this might sting a little.

288
00:18:10.922 --> 00:18:13.689
I wanna see you again
real soon.

289
00:18:15.265 --> 00:18:17.633
[Wilson]
Virtual colonoscopy was clean.

290
00:18:17.704 --> 00:18:19.765
No colon cancer.

291
00:18:19.841 --> 00:18:21.970
What happened to our regular,
old-fashioned colonoscopy?

292
00:18:22.046 --> 00:18:25.952
She was uncomfortable
doing any more tests.
I had to convince her to do that one.

293
00:18:26.021 --> 00:18:30.086
Do you get that often?
Women would rather die
than get naked with you?

294
00:18:30.163 --> 00:18:32.429
She's scared.

295
00:18:33.504 --> 00:18:37.672
But not of tests.
Just embarrassing ones.

296
00:18:37.746 --> 00:18:39.476
Yeah.

297
00:18:43.625 --> 00:18:48.329
It's not an inflammatory process.
It's not a clot, because Chase's
angio says so.

298
00:18:49.403 --> 00:18:53.036
And it's not cancer,
because her tush is perfect.

299
00:18:53.111 --> 00:18:56.949
Anybody else got an Aunt Elyssa
with weird stuff?

300
00:18:59.091 --> 00:19:01.391
Maybe it's worth looking into-

301
00:19:01.463 --> 00:19:03.695
I thought you said
Carly's angio was clean.

302
00:19:03.767 --> 00:19:07.206
It was clean.

303
00:19:14.623 --> 00:19:16.559
You guys see
the problem here?

304
00:19:18.732 --> 00:19:21.863
There's no indication
of any abnormalities.

305
00:19:21.939 --> 00:19:23.875
No lesions,
no spurs, no mastis-

306
00:19:23.944 --> 00:19:28.442
Her toes are screwed up.
They're backwards.

307
00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.856
You guys know how much surgery
it's gonna take to swap 'em back?

308
00:19:31.926 --> 00:19:33.690
What are you talking about?

309
00:19:33.763 --> 00:19:37.897
Well, either she literally
has two left feet,
or you angioed the wrong leg.

310
00:19:45.556 --> 00:19:47.719
That's impossible.
It can't be-
Or maybe it was Jenny.

311
00:19:47.793 --> 00:19:51.756
How come some resident
signed this radiology report?
Were you even in the room?

312
00:19:51.836 --> 00:19:53.897
- I'll redo her angio-
- You'll do nothing!

313
00:19:59.385 --> 00:20:01.947
Foreman,
you do the angiogram.

314
00:20:14.282 --> 00:20:16.218
I can't believe I did that.

315
00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:23.625
Why do we have to
redo the angiogram?

316
00:20:23.703 --> 00:20:27.142
There was a shadow
on the first test result.
Shadow?

317
00:20:28.446 --> 00:20:30.473
A shadow means
there could be a blood clot, right?

318
00:20:32.153 --> 00:20:35.182
I read Conn's Current Therapy.

319
00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:39.064
A real page-turner.
No, it wasn't that kind of shadow.

320
00:20:41.574 --> 00:20:44.876
My chest hurts.
It's from the tracer I injected.

321
00:20:44.948 --> 00:20:49.788
You might also get a little nauseous
or have a metallic taste.
All normal.

322
00:20:49.858 --> 00:20:52.921
I'm a runner.
I shouldn't feel like this.

323
00:20:52.997 --> 00:20:55.297
Carly, I'm looking
at your vitals right now and-

324
00:20:55.369 --> 00:20:57.362
I can't breathe.
Carly?

325
00:20:59.144 --> 00:21:00.772
My chest.

326
00:21:04.155 --> 00:21:06.091
My chest.

327
00:21:09.533 --> 00:21:11.799
Respiratory arrest.
Call code.

328
00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.102
- What do you got?
- She's drowning.

329
00:21:31.947 --> 00:21:34.919
[Cameron]
Foreman did a thoracentesis
to drain the fluid from her lungs.

330
00:21:34.986 --> 00:21:38.516
She's stable.
They sent the fluid to the lab.
It'll be back in a few hours.

331
00:21:39.596 --> 00:21:43.331
You'll be happy to know
Chase's mistake didn't cost her.
Angio revealed no clot.

332
00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:46.576
I'm thrilled.

333
00:22:52.249 --> 00:22:54.617
[Monitor Beeping]

334
00:23:32.701 --> 00:23:34.898
Okay, see, now you'rejust
being stubborn.

335
00:23:34.972 --> 00:23:37.636
It's cold.
It's a perfectly good excuse
to wear your lab coat.

336
00:23:37.711 --> 00:23:39.647
Carly needs a heart transplant.

337
00:23:41.052 --> 00:23:43.682
Thoracentesis revealed
a transudate?

338
00:23:43.758 --> 00:23:45.921
I haven't gotten it back yet.

339
00:23:45.996 --> 00:23:49.264
Her MUGA scan,
what was the ejection fraction?

340
00:23:49.336 --> 00:23:53.106
Maybe you could treat it surgically.
I haven't done the MUGA.

341
00:23:53.178 --> 00:23:55.478
How do you know
she needs a heart transplant?

342
00:23:56.584 --> 00:23:58.520
I got my aura read today.

343
00:23:58.589 --> 00:24:01.059
It said someone close to me
had a broken heart.

344
00:24:01.127 --> 00:24:03.563
Since when do I need
the secret pass code to talk to you?

345
00:24:03.633 --> 00:24:07.003
I can't tell you anything.
Professional responsibility.

346
00:24:07.073 --> 00:24:09.134
Like that matters to you.

347
00:24:09.211 --> 00:24:12.183
Not my professional
responsibility, yours.

348
00:24:12.251 --> 00:24:14.778
New regime.
You gotta keep
your head down too.

349
00:24:14.857 --> 00:24:17.419
Now that's good thinking,
because I was gonna go
right to Cuddy...

350
00:24:17.496 --> 00:24:19.432
and rat you out
as soon as you were done talking.

351
00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.834
I'm not saying you want to.
I'm saying you'd be obligated to.

352
00:24:21.904 --> 00:24:24.340
Because of my position
on the board?

353
00:24:28.486 --> 00:24:30.922
Because of my position
on the transplant committee?

354
00:24:33.362 --> 00:24:36.266
Hey, you brought this up for a reason.
You need to talk to me.

355
00:24:36.335 --> 00:24:38.703
I can't.

356
00:24:42.983 --> 00:24:44.816
You sure you're doing the right thing?

357
00:24:44.887 --> 00:24:48.291
I've come up with a few
really great rationalizations.

358
00:24:48.361 --> 00:24:51.299
Sorry to interrupt.
We have a problem.

359
00:24:51.367 --> 00:24:53.564
[Cameron]
Thoracentesis revealed a transudate.

360
00:24:53.639 --> 00:24:56.907
We did an echo.
She's in severe congestive heart failure.

361
00:24:56.978 --> 00:25:00.246
She needs a heart transplant.

362
00:25:01.555 --> 00:25:04.925
- We'll get her on the list immediately.
- She's already on the list.

363
00:25:10.373 --> 00:25:13.675
- [Knocks]
- Come in.

364
00:25:15.117 --> 00:25:17.918
Thanks.

365
00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:21.053
What is a department
of Diagnostic Medicine?

366
00:25:22.933 --> 00:25:24.960
That's Dr. House's department.

367
00:25:25.038 --> 00:25:28.499
They deal with cases
that other doctors can't figure out.

368
00:25:29.581 --> 00:25:31.710
It's a financial black hole.

369
00:25:31.785 --> 00:25:33.982
The department costs us
three million a year...

370
00:25:34.057 --> 00:25:36.550
to treat one patient a week.

371
00:25:36.629 --> 00:25:38.599
He saves one patient per week.
What about everyone else?

372
00:25:38.667 --> 00:25:41.103
His department's not going to find
the cure for breast cancer.

373
00:25:41.171 --> 00:25:44.541
- Uh, maybe not, but-
- Are you sleeping with House?

374
00:25:44.612 --> 00:25:48.347
What? No.
But you did. Right?

375
00:25:48.421 --> 00:25:51.154
A long time ago?

376
00:25:51.227 --> 00:25:53.288
That's an incredibly
inappropriate question.

377
00:25:53.364 --> 00:25:57.771
If your judgment is compromised
by a prior or current relationship,
that is my business.

378
00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:00.310
I respect him.
That is all you need to know.

379
00:26:00.379 --> 00:26:02.815
He's still not wearing the coat.

380
00:26:02.885 --> 00:26:05.652
Well, I told him-
I'm sure you did.

381
00:26:05.724 --> 00:26:10.325
And yet he's not wearing it.
I'm just wondering if that's
a reflection on him...

382
00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:12.427
or on you.

383
00:26:15.411 --> 00:26:17.677
[Monitor Beeping]

384
00:26:26.066 --> 00:26:28.002
You're Dr. House.

385
00:26:28.071 --> 00:26:31.510
I found a picture online
of you at a conference.
You need a heart transplant.

386
00:26:32.914 --> 00:26:35.384
- I run. I work out-
- You cut yourself.

387
00:26:37.257 --> 00:26:39.727
Probably highly ritualized.

388
00:26:39.796 --> 00:26:43.326
You play the same Sarah McLachlan song
over and over while you do it.

389
00:26:43.403 --> 00:26:45.703
Probably works better
than antidepressants.

390
00:26:46.843 --> 00:26:51.250
- I don't understand how that-
- You're a high-powered bulimic.
You make yourself throw up.

391
00:26:52.322 --> 00:26:57.663
You had to find the most efficient way
to vomit without revealing
the telltale signs ofbulimia,

392
00:26:57.734 --> 00:27:01.332
which is all, ooh,
very unseemly for a C.E.O.

393
00:27:01.408 --> 00:27:06.350
So, you found a common antidote
to accidental poisoning to do the job-

394
00:27:06.418 --> 00:27:08.354
Ipecac.

395
00:27:08.422 --> 00:27:11.553
Which is great if your kid's
just swallowed a bottle of aspirin,

396
00:27:11.629 --> 00:27:14.031
but really, really bad
if it's a habit.

397
00:27:15.337 --> 00:27:17.273
It causes muscle damage.

398
00:27:17.341 --> 00:27:19.504
It caused the pain in your leg.

399
00:27:22.118 --> 00:27:24.486
And destroyed your heart.

400
00:27:27.128 --> 00:27:29.621
How often do you do it?

401
00:27:35.212 --> 00:27:37.648
Three times a week.

402
00:27:40.824 --> 00:27:45.425
In about an hour,
there's gonna be an emergency meeting
of the transplant committee...

403
00:27:45.501 --> 00:27:49.202
to discuss where you fall on the list
should a new heart become available.

404
00:27:49.276 --> 00:27:53.980
Problem is, I am required
to tell the committee about your bulimia.

405
00:27:54.052 --> 00:27:59.222
It's a major psychiatric condition.
Ranks right up there with suicidal.
Makes you a very bad risk.

406
00:27:59.297 --> 00:28:03.533
So, you're here to tell me
I have just a few hours to live?

407
00:28:05.175 --> 00:28:07.304
Unless I lie to the committee.

408
00:28:09.284 --> 00:28:12.586
But if they find out,
I lose my medical license.

409
00:28:15.230 --> 00:28:18.464
This would be a very good time
to offer me a bribe.

410
00:28:18.537 --> 00:28:21.099
You know, how much is your life worth?
How much is my job worth?

411
00:28:21.176 --> 00:28:26.380
Why are you here
doing this to me?
What do you want?

412
00:28:26.454 --> 00:28:30.918
I wanna know what's right.

413
00:28:30.997 --> 00:28:33.024
Am I worth it?

414
00:28:34.771 --> 00:28:36.707
You think I'm pathetic.

415
00:28:37.778 --> 00:28:40.648
Has a goodjob.
Everything in the world.

416
00:28:40.717 --> 00:28:43.553
But she just doesn't like
the way that she looks.

417
00:28:43.623 --> 00:28:45.923
Oh, stop hiding!

418
00:28:45.995 --> 00:28:49.126
I'm asking you
if you wanna live or die?
You can't even say that.

419
00:28:49.201 --> 00:28:52.071
What do you
want me to do? Cry?
Yes.

420
00:28:52.142 --> 00:28:55.603
I want you to tell me
that your life is important to you,
because I don't know.

421
00:28:57.987 --> 00:29:00.457
[Sighs]

422
00:29:00.526 --> 00:29:05.764
Because that's what's
on the table right now-
your life.

423
00:29:22.505 --> 00:29:24.668
I don't wanna die.

424
00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:29.986
I don't.

425
00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:41.165
This 32-year-old female
was admitted by my staff...

426
00:29:41.245 --> 00:29:43.978
because of paralysis
and pain in her right thigh.

427
00:29:44.050 --> 00:29:48.754
The patient rapidly deteriorated
and now has severe congestive
heart failure.

428
00:29:48.828 --> 00:29:51.800
Pressors and vasodilators
have not improved
her condition whatsoever.

429
00:29:51.867 --> 00:29:55.739
Pulmonary function tests
show an F.E.C. Of over three liters,

430
00:29:55.808 --> 00:29:59.543
with F.E. V. -1
of at least 90% of predicted...

431
00:29:59.617 --> 00:30:04.321
and preserved F.E.D. /F.E.C. Ratio
and preserved D.L.C.O. As well.

432
00:30:04.394 --> 00:30:10.270
Her MUGA had an ejection fraction of 19%
with no focal wall motion abnormalities.

433
00:30:10.339 --> 00:30:15.407
Heart catheterization revealed
clean left, right and circumflex arteries.

434
00:30:15.483 --> 00:30:19.685
And a subsequent biopsy revealed
irreversible cardiomyopathy.

435
00:30:19.759 --> 00:30:22.059
Which is why we're here.

436
00:30:22.131 --> 00:30:25.866
Uh, Dr. House, I'm confused
by your time and date stamps.

437
00:30:25.939 --> 00:30:29.640
It appears that you put Carly
on the transplant list
before you did these tests.

438
00:30:32.286 --> 00:30:34.688
I had a hunch.

439
00:30:34.758 --> 00:30:37.388
You don't have hunches.
You know.

440
00:30:37.464 --> 00:30:40.630
Look, if the tests
had come back differently,
obviously I would have taken her off the list.

441
00:30:40.703 --> 00:30:42.639
But on the long shot-

442
00:30:45.046 --> 00:30:49.419
On the long shot that I was right,
I didn't want to waste time.

443
00:30:52.728 --> 00:30:55.062
Is there any exclusion criteria
we should know about?

444
00:30:57.406 --> 00:31:01.506
CAT scan revealed no tumors,
and Dr. Wilson found no trace of cancer.

445
00:31:04.086 --> 00:31:05.953
What about any other criteria?

446
00:31:06.023 --> 00:31:09.359
- No atherosclerotic vascular disease.
- Are there any-

447
00:31:09.431 --> 00:31:14.135
No pneumonia, no bacteremia.
No hep "B" or "C"
or any other letters.

448
00:31:14.207 --> 00:31:16.302
Substance abuse.
Any history-

449
00:31:16.379 --> 00:31:18.315
No alcohol. No drugs.

450
00:31:18.383 --> 00:31:22.585
Any psychiatric conditions?
History of depression?
She's a little blue.

451
00:31:22.659 --> 00:31:26.599
But turns out
she needs a heart transplant, so-

452
00:31:33.915 --> 00:31:36.613
Dr. House, if you subvert
or mislead this committee,

453
00:31:36.688 --> 00:31:39.592
you will be subject
to disciplinary action.

454
00:31:44.671 --> 00:31:48.041
Dr. Cuddy, do you have reason
to think that I would lie?

455
00:31:48.112 --> 00:31:51.676
I simply want you
to answer the question.

456
00:31:51.753 --> 00:31:55.157
Is there anything
on the recipient exclusion criteria...

457
00:31:55.227 --> 00:31:59.395
that would disqualify your patient
from getting a heart?

458
00:32:10.158 --> 00:32:12.492
No.

459
00:32:14.735 --> 00:32:17.103
[Rolling Thunder]

460
00:32:19.679 --> 00:32:22.583
Beautiful organ donor weather.

461
00:32:22.651 --> 00:32:24.587
You lied, didn't you?

462
00:32:24.656 --> 00:32:27.058
I never lie.
Big mistake.

463
00:32:27.127 --> 00:32:29.997
Then you should have voted
against putting her on the list.

464
00:32:30.068 --> 00:32:34.372
- You're my friend.
- Oh, geez, have some backbone.

465
00:32:34.443 --> 00:32:36.379
If you think I'm wrong,
do something.

466
00:32:36.448 --> 00:32:38.611
Wait. You're getting mad at me
for sticking up for you?

467
00:32:38.685 --> 00:32:42.682
You value our friendship
more than your ethical responsibilities.

468
00:32:42.761 --> 00:32:45.562
Our friendship
is an ethical responsibility.

469
00:32:46.636 --> 00:32:48.868
[Pager Beeping]

470
00:32:50.711 --> 00:32:52.544
What is it?

471
00:32:54.051 --> 00:32:56.453
My patient's getting a heart.
[Thunderclap]

472
00:33:00.999 --> 00:33:02.935
[Whirring]

473
00:33:09.183 --> 00:33:11.153
[Cameron]
He's not gonna fire you.

474
00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:13.588
I'd fire you. Bye-bye.

475
00:33:15.363 --> 00:33:18.631
I screw up, the patient dies,
I'll never get another job.

476
00:33:18.704 --> 00:33:21.608
So stick your head between your legs
and go lick your wounds in Gstaad.

477
00:33:22.945 --> 00:33:24.972
Well, I like it here.

478
00:33:31.297 --> 00:33:34.463
You guys don't think it's weird
House knew the patient needed
a heart transplant...

479
00:33:34.536 --> 00:33:36.506
before we did any heart tests?

480
00:33:37.644 --> 00:33:39.705
That's House.
He knows things.

481
00:33:39.781 --> 00:33:41.648
But usually he's putting it
in our face.

482
00:33:41.718 --> 00:33:43.847
Telling us how cleverly
he figured it out.

483
00:33:43.923 --> 00:33:46.827
This time nothing.
Just had a hunch.

484
00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:51.036
That is weird.

485
00:34:20.768 --> 00:34:23.398
[Monitor Beeping]

486
00:34:23.474 --> 00:34:25.774
Cauterize that one bleeder.

487
00:34:30.588 --> 00:34:32.785
Okay, ready for the donor heart.

488
00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:37.130
[Monitor Beeping,
Sustained Tone]

489
00:34:38.304 --> 00:34:41.640
They just stopped Carly's heart,
and your dumb patient-

490
00:34:41.712 --> 00:34:44.650
They're all-
Oh, the guy who can't talk.
Mr. Van Der Meer.

491
00:34:44.718 --> 00:34:47.815
He's scheduled an appointment
to see you.
Oh, goody.

492
00:34:50.196 --> 00:34:54.900
I wanted you to know
Chase is worried
you're going to fire him.

493
00:34:54.973 --> 00:34:57.809
It's bad enough
that screwups cost lives.

494
00:34:57.879 --> 00:35:00.042
Now we've got Vogler,
screwups cost jobs.

495
00:35:00.117 --> 00:35:02.212
I want Chase scared.

496
00:35:02.289 --> 00:35:04.885
I want him doing everything he can
to protect his job.

497
00:35:04.961 --> 00:35:08.833
Dr. House, if you were in his position,
wouldn't you be more likely
to perform well if you were reassured-

498
00:35:08.902 --> 00:35:13.275
Oh, will you stop it
with the book?
Why are you doing this?

499
00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.352
I'm not doing anything.
You're manipulating everyone.

500
00:35:20.293 --> 00:35:24.131
People... dismiss me.

501
00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:28.700
Because I'm a woman.
Because I'm pretty.

502
00:35:28.777 --> 00:35:32.238
Because... I'm not aggressive.

503
00:35:33.621 --> 00:35:37.493
My opinions shouldn't be rejected
just because people don't like me.

504
00:35:37.563 --> 00:35:39.259
They like you.

505
00:35:40.569 --> 00:35:42.471
Everyone likes you.

506
00:35:44.477 --> 00:35:46.241
Do you?

507
00:36:05.588 --> 00:36:07.558
I have to know.

508
00:36:08.862 --> 00:36:10.160
No.

509
00:36:18.582 --> 00:36:20.518
Okay.

510
00:36:35.585 --> 00:36:38.523
[Chattering]

511
00:36:38.592 --> 00:36:42.327
Five hours, 23 minutes.
That's fast.

512
00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:44.927
Is that good or bad?
It depends.

513
00:36:45.005 --> 00:36:48.307
Either surgery went really well,
or it ended really abruptly.

514
00:36:51.352 --> 00:36:54.222
Textbook.
She'll outlive us all.

515
00:36:55.393 --> 00:36:57.329
Thank you.

516
00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:05.640
[Man On P.A.]
Triage nurse, pick up line 2741.

517
00:37:05.715 --> 00:37:08.619
[Door Opens]

518
00:37:08.688 --> 00:37:11.819
So, sing for me.

519
00:37:15.035 --> 00:37:17.437
Oh, no, no, no, no.
Come on, look.

520
00:37:17.507 --> 00:37:21.379
When you had your surgery,
you were intubated. The surgeon
stuck a tube down your throat.

521
00:37:21.448 --> 00:37:24.614
Now, it never happens
and it's never caught,
but it happened.

522
00:37:24.689 --> 00:37:29.222
Your vocal cords were paralyzed.
I treated the spastic dysphonia
with Botox.

523
00:37:29.298 --> 00:37:32.566
Ironically, a substance
that tightens every other
corner of your face.

524
00:37:32.639 --> 00:37:36.670
It actually loosens the tongue.
I have healed you.
You can talk.

525
00:37:39.086 --> 00:37:41.147
[Sighs]

526
00:37:42.593 --> 00:37:44.529
Oh, well.

527
00:37:44.597 --> 00:37:46.567
Boo!

528
00:37:47.837 --> 00:37:50.741
[Sighs]
Okay.

529
00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:53.907
You don't have to say anything.
It can be our little secret.

530
00:37:53.983 --> 00:37:57.444
If you can talk, blink twice.

531
00:38:00.363 --> 00:38:02.731
But you're not going to,

532
00:38:02.802 --> 00:38:07.369
because you think
you won't be entitled to the money
you won in the settlement with Simpson.

533
00:38:10.117 --> 00:38:12.485
Yesterday, I would have said
you had to give the money back.

534
00:38:12.556 --> 00:38:17.658
Today, hospital's
come into a lot of money.

535
00:38:17.734 --> 00:38:19.670
Mum's the word.

536
00:38:30.194 --> 00:38:33.257
[Door Opens]

537
00:38:56.148 --> 00:38:58.311
Hey.

538
00:38:58.386 --> 00:39:00.322
Hey.

539
00:39:02.829 --> 00:39:06.097
I know the cardiologist
has given you some guidelines,

540
00:39:06.169 --> 00:39:09.335
a schedule of medication
and a strict diet.

541
00:39:09.409 --> 00:39:13.942
Just what somebody
with an eating disorder needs.

542
00:39:14.019 --> 00:39:16.752
So I thought I'd get you started.

543
00:39:16.825 --> 00:39:19.295
Fried chicken
from the Carnegie Deli.

544
00:39:21.702 --> 00:39:24.104
- You're kidding.
- Yeah.

545
00:39:24.173 --> 00:39:26.109
Actually, I got it downstairs.

546
00:39:26.178 --> 00:39:27.874
[Chuckles]

547
00:39:31.322 --> 00:39:34.726
Why did you fight for me?

548
00:39:34.796 --> 00:39:38.497
You risked so much,
and you hardly know me.

549
00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:46.286
You're my patient.

550
00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:53.798
Don't screw it up.

551
00:40:02.388 --> 00:40:06.488
~~ [Music]

552
00:40:28.676 --> 00:40:32.582
~~ [Continues]

553
00:40:39.466 --> 00:40:41.595
Love this part!

554
00:40:45.679 --> 00:40:47.774
~~ [Stops]

555
00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:51.254
Okay. He ruined it.

556
00:40:51.324 --> 00:40:55.628
Just wanted to stop by
and introduce myself. I'm Edward Vogler,
new chairman of the board.

557
00:40:55.700 --> 00:40:57.636
In a way, I guess
that makes me your boss.

558
00:40:59.541 --> 00:41:03.845
I am sorry
about the lab coat thing.
My dry cleaners just destroyed it.

559
00:41:05.654 --> 00:41:09.252
That was my very first
heart transplant committee meeting.
Very exciting.

560
00:41:09.329 --> 00:41:12.631
Trust me,
Six Flags- way more exciting.

561
00:41:12.703 --> 00:41:16.837
Your patient's very lucky
to have such a passionate doctor.
Stands up for what he believes in.

562
00:41:16.912 --> 00:41:18.882
Sweet of you to say.

563
00:41:18.949 --> 00:41:21.283
Yeah. Afraid
you've been duped though.

564
00:41:21.355 --> 00:41:25.659
The nurse found this
in the patient's purse.

565
00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:33.310
Oh, my.
If only I'd known.

566
00:41:33.380 --> 00:41:36.819
Tough being a doctor,
having all that power.

567
00:41:36.887 --> 00:41:38.823
The power to play God.

568
00:41:38.891 --> 00:41:42.922
Yes. I don't envy
the transplant committee
their responsibility.

569
00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:46.770
They basically
would have been forced
to kill that poor girl.

570
00:41:46.841 --> 00:41:48.936
I'm not sure
I could have done that.

571
00:41:50.516 --> 00:41:53.043
This is not a game to me,
Dr. House.

572
00:41:53.121 --> 00:41:58.086
No. It's actually
more like we're dancing
right now.

573
00:41:58.165 --> 00:42:00.192
So let's get to the point.
You don't like me.

574
00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:03.903
I'm pretty sure
I'm not gonna like you.
It's nothing personal.

575
00:42:03.977 --> 00:42:06.641
I don't like anybody.

576
00:42:06.717 --> 00:42:08.653
But none of that
really matters, does it?

577
00:42:08.721 --> 00:42:11.454
Because you've got money,
and I've got tenure.

578
00:42:11.526 --> 00:42:15.762
You need full board approval
to get rid of me.
I've got Cuddy.

579
00:42:15.836 --> 00:42:18.306
Right.
And Wilson.

580
00:42:18.374 --> 00:42:21.972
So, as long as
we're stuck with each other,

581
00:42:22.049 --> 00:42:24.246
we might as well
ignore each other.

582
00:42:24.321 --> 00:42:27.589
~~ [Different Music]

583
00:42:28.662 --> 00:42:30.598
~~ [Stops]

584
00:42:30.667 --> 00:42:32.694
That wasn't nearly as dramatic
as I was hoping.

585
00:42:32.772 --> 00:42:34.331
[Chuckles]

586
00:42:36.613 --> 00:42:39.585
I looked into that tenure thing,
and you're right.

587
00:42:39.653 --> 00:42:43.320
It's actually easier for me
to get rid of a board member
like Cuddy or Wilson...

588
00:42:43.394 --> 00:42:45.830
than to get rid of a doctor.

589
00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:49.031
That's interesting, isn't it?

590
00:44:11.714 --> 00:44:12.977
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0.500 --> 00:00:2.00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>

